1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,720
 Hello everyone, good morning, welcome to the second session of today's lectures.

2
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:20,580
 So here we I am going to talk about supervised machine learning which we started discussing

3
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:28,840
 last week and today particularly I am going to cover decision tree and random forest.

4
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:32,000
 So before that let us just get a quick recap.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,060
 I know that you have been working on this even through the hands-on tutorial.

6
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:44,520
 You have been looking at the classification problems but still just a little recollection.

7
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:55,720
 So classification is essentially a mapping between set of features to a set of level

8
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,760
 or a particular level.

9
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:06,680
 So in case of binary classification for example we have a record where each record has x which

10
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:13,880
 are the attributes or features or independent variable whatever we may want to call it and

11
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:22,520
 y is the class or the level or the output and in binary y is either 0 or 1.

12
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:31,040
 In case of multi-class classification y can take more than two classes.

13
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:37,360
 Classification task is to learn a model that maps the attribute set to the corresponding

14
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:45,120
 class level so that whenever a new data point comes we can immediately through the model

15
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:55,480
 we can immediately predict what is the likely level for this particular input test.

16
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:02,260
 Now if you recall last week we have talked about three classifiers, knife base, nearest

17
00:02:02,260 --> 00:02:05,800
 neighbor and support vector machines.

18
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:13,160
 Today we are going to talk about decision tree and we would talk about ensemble classifiers.

19
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:19,320
 Example network or deep neural network which is like more popularly known as deep learning

20
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:24,240
 that will be covered in subsequent lectures.

21
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:27,440
 So let us first look at decision tree.

22
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:34,920
 So before that let us take an example classification problem and this classification problem is

23
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:37,440
 a simple problem.

24
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:45,240
 So here we want to decide whether to play tennis on a particular day, so this is our

25
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:50,560
 model or this is the model which we want to build that given the weather condition of

26
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:57,160
 a particular day the model is going to predict whether there will be a tennis match or not.

27
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:03,960
 Now in order to train this model let us say the club where the tennis is being played

28
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:12,440
 they have collected last 14 days of data, the weather condition on those 14 days and

29
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:17,600
 the corresponding status like whether actually a match was played or not.

30
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:25,880
 So as you can see for example you look at day 1, day 1, in day 1 the outlook was sunny

31
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:33,800
 temperature was hot, humidity was high and wind was weak and on that day there was no

32
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:37,280
 game which was played.

33
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:46,960
 On the other hand if you look at day 4 outlook was rain, temperature was mild, humidity was

34
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:54,240
 high, wind was weak on that day a match was played.

35
00:03:54,240 --> 00:04:02,560
 So similarly you have such a history of 14 days and every day what was the weather condition

36
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:07,200
 and whether a match was played or not.

37
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:16,520
 Now since we are dealing with two levels here, one is whether a match was played or another

38
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:23,320
 is whether the match was not played yes or no, so this is a binary classification problem

39
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:33,360
 and this outlook, temperature, humidity and wind these four are the features.

40
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:41,120
 So they are also called categorical features because they can pick values from certain

41
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:42,280
 fixed categories.

42
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:51,120
 So for example the outlook can be sunny, overcast or rain, temperature can be hot, mild or cool.

43
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:57,000
 So similar like that, it can also have numerical features, now in this example there was no

44
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,560
 such example of numerical features but let us say if instead of bucketizing the temperature

45
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:09,960
 as like hot or mild or cool, if we just use the centigrade temperature like exactly the

46
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:16,920
 temperature is less than 20 degree centigrade or 30 degree centigrade or 40 degree centigrade

47
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:22,480
 in that case it would have been a numerical feature.

48
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:30,520
 Now with this background let us see how a decision tree can be built using this training

49
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:33,720
 data which we have.

50
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:43,120
 So essentially we are going to construct a decision tree like this that where this is

51
00:05:43,120 --> 00:05:52,400
 a node, so this is also called a root node, these are internal nodes and these are the

52
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:56,160
 leaf nodes.

53
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:04,280
 So decision tree essentially enforce a hierarchical hierarchy of rules to make a prediction.

54
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:14,520
 So this internal nodes and the root node they test the rule and in the leaf node you see

55
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:18,960
 that ultimate decision is there.

56
00:06:18,960 --> 00:06:26,640
 So decision tree learning is essentially learning such a tree from the level training data as

57
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:32,660
 we saw earlier those like 14 days of match data whether a match was played or not depending

58
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:37,960
 on the weather condition.

59
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:43,320
 Now the basic idea behind decision tree is generally very simple.

60
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:51,800
 We would like to recursively partition the entire training data into homogeneous regions.

61
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:56,140
 Now what is meant by homogeneous region?

62
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:03,000
 homogeneous region is one where all the training input will have the same level.

63
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,080
 So let us just see one example.

64
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:11,760
 So let us say this is our entire training data with the corresponding levels.

65
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:18,800
 So here as the picture suggests there are three levels red, blue and green and the training

66
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:26,040
 data like all these dots denote a particular data point.

67
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:35,000
 Now the goal of or in this case a homogeneous region is to divide the entire training space

68
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 or the data space into regions where only one type of level is present.

69
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:50,640
 So for example here this is one homogeneous region because here there is only blue levels.

70
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:56,160
 Similarly here there is there are only red data points.

71
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:04,200
 Similarly you can continuously divide and eventually after this division like we have

72
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:10,600
 just divided the entire training data space into smaller subspaces which we are calling

73
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:17,960
 as homogeneous region where essentially there is just like one single class level data with

74
00:08:17,960 --> 00:08:21,560
 a single class level is present.

75
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:29,920
 Now do we always want such a scenario like where all the points are just one class level?

76
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:37,760
 Ideally yes but in reality they may still have some training data point which do not

77
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:45,240
 belonging to the exactly one class level but the majority of the levels in a particular

78
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:54,120
 region will be of one type and that constitutes a homogeneous region.

79
00:08:54,120 --> 00:09:02,640
 So now if we can let us say divide the entire training data into such homogeneous regions

80
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:10,880
 in that case within each group we can simply fit a supervised learner and that supervised

81
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:15,520
 learner is going to predict the majority level.

82
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:25,800
 Now we will come or we will discuss this in more detail a little later.

83
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:36,080
 Now let us look at what happens when a particular test input is given and how its level is predicted.

84
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:46,120
 So let us say that we have divided the whole region into this homogeneous regions and essentially

85
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:52,840
 homogeneous region means that each of the this black lines which have created the homogeneous

86
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:57,240
 regions they correspond to some rule.

87
00:09:57,240 --> 00:10:08,480
 So the first rule is let us say that in terms of feature 1 or X1 our this line this black

88
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:17,040
 line correspond to this rule whether X1 value is greater than 3.5 or not.

89
00:10:17,040 --> 00:10:27,600
 You see that actually this rule divides the entire space into this part and this part.

90
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:31,200
 Now of course this itself is not sufficient.

91
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:36,240
 So you need to also consider the feature 2 or X2.

92
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:43,000
 So my first line here the line here this correspond to X2 is equal to 2.

93
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:49,480
 So we are defining another rule whether X2 is greater than 2 or not.

94
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:56,800
 If yes then we are talking about this region if no we are talking about this region.

95
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:03,120
 Now if no we are talking about this region then this entire thing is only red.

96
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:13,900
 So if a particular input falls here then we will immediately predict that has to be red.

97
00:11:13,900 --> 00:11:21,760
 On the other hand if you look at this particular region in this region X1 is greater than 3.5

98
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:23,880
 X2 is also greater than 2.

99
00:11:23,880 --> 00:11:38,540
 So X1 greater than sorry this should be X1 greater less than 3.5 it is not greater than

100
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:40,800
 so sorry for the typo.

101
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:50,040
 So if X1 is less than 3.5 and X2 is greater than 2 we are essentially in this particular

102
00:11:50,040 --> 00:11:51,040
 region.

103
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:55,160
 So there are a lot of red dots there.

104
00:11:55,160 --> 00:12:10,000
 So let me just change the color a little bit or also just like remove this.

105
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:24,920
 So essentially we have now yeah so we have one rule whether X1 is less than 3.5 so that

106
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:28,160
 actually that correspond to this particular line.

107
00:12:28,160 --> 00:12:34,240
 So this divides the entire space into two regions the right hand side and the left hand

108
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:35,240
 side.

109
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:41,160
 Now in the left hand side now our first rule is this line so this line correspond to X2

110
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:42,520
 is equal to 2.

111
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:48,360
 So if X2 is greater than 2 then we are talking about this region if X2 is less than 2 we

112
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:51,600
 are talking about this region correct.

113
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:59,120
 Now if X2 is greater than 2 in that case X2 is greater than 2 and X1 is less than 3.5

114
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:04,080
 so that is this particular region.

115
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:11,240
 So if any point comes here in that case it will be predicted as red.

116
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:17,800
 When X1 is less than 3.5 and X2 is greater than 2 then we are talking about this particular

117
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:24,560
 region and as the color suggest that region is green.

118
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:33,880
 So now let us move to the right part so this correspond to X1 which is okay I am I think

119
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:36,440
 sorry I am just making some silly mistakes.

120
00:13:36,440 --> 00:13:43,120
 So essentially this is actually 3.5 more than 3.5 so I just like didn't notice the yes

121
00:13:43,120 --> 00:13:44,720
 and no level.

122
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:53,240
 So when X1 is 3.5 we are essentially in this region and in that region if X2 is greater

123
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:56,000
 than 3 that means this.

124
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:02,960
 So this is dividing it into again 2 regions the one lower and the one upper.

125
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:10,520
 In the lower region our X1 value is greater than 3.5 and X2 value is greater than 3.

126
00:14:10,520 --> 00:14:18,240
 So in that case we are predicting so in that case we are predicting green otherwise we

127
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:28,640
 are predicting red.

128
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:35,840
 Now let us see for a particular test input what happens in this case the test input is

129
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:43,400
 actually belonging to the green point like the test input is denoted as the black dot

130
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:46,480
 here.

131
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:53,400
 Now after this decision 3 is created now I am just going to predict what happens with

132
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:54,960
 the test input.

133
00:14:54,960 --> 00:15:00,760
 Now see the test input the X1 value is greater than 3.5 that is not correct.

134
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:06,880
 So it goes to the left hand side X2 is greater than 2 that is yes.

135
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:14,040
 So in that case this actually followed this path and we make the decision that here the

136
00:15:14,040 --> 00:15:18,980
 class level is green.

137
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:27,120
 So you see that once the decision tree is constructed properly it is a very efficient

138
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:30,720
 algorithm at the test time.

139
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:39,400
 So if you look at this particular example when a particular test input has come we just

140
00:15:39,400 --> 00:15:48,560
 made 2 comparisons we checked whether a particular feature X1 whether it is greater than 3.5

141
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:54,080
 and whether another feature X2 whether that is greater than 2 that is it.

142
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:58,640
 Only 2 feature value comparisons we have made.

143
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:05,440
 On the other hand if we were to use something like a K nearest neighbor the K nearest neighbor

144
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:09,640
 would have compared distances from all the training input.

145
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:16,400
 So that way it is much, much costly at the test time because if there are in this example

146
00:16:16,400 --> 00:16:19,400
 there are so many training inputs.

147
00:16:19,400 --> 00:16:26,040
 Now if a particular test input has come in that case the test input would have been compared

148
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:31,880
 with all the training inputs then we would have found the nearest let us say 5 or 6 neighbors

149
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:38,200
 or 7 neighbors and then picked the level which is majority in the neighborhood.

150
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:44,140
 But the initial comparison to figure out who are the actual neighbors that would have

151
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:50,520
 costed a lot of time.

152
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:56,760
 Now here the root node essentially actually consists of all the training input and it

153
00:16:56,760 --> 00:17:04,200
 decides like where to go as we move down the tree each internal node also deals with a

154
00:17:04,200 --> 00:17:10,720
 subset of training inputs and effectively the leaf nodes deal with the least possible

155
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:17,240
 training input where there is not much variability in terms of the class levels.

156
00:17:17,240 --> 00:17:26,480
 So mostly the leaf nodes we try to make them deal with only homogeneous regions.

157
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:31,880
 Now there are still some considerations when we are building decision trees what should

158
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:35,000
 be the size of the shape of the tree.

159
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:43,360
 So for example like what should be the number of internal nodes and leaf nodes.

160
00:17:43,360 --> 00:17:51,000
 So see that there is no rule saying that you have to only have two decisions against a

161
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:54,200
 particular internal node.

162
00:17:54,200 --> 00:18:01,960
 So you could have had another decision like this.

163
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:10,560
 So it is not necessary that every tree just need to have like two decisions.

164
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:17,240
 So that is one the branching factor of internal node as I mentioned.

165
00:18:17,240 --> 00:18:20,880
 Then how many leaf nodes do you want to have?

166
00:18:20,880 --> 00:18:23,480
 How many internal nodes do you want to have?

167
00:18:23,480 --> 00:18:26,040
 What is going to be the depth of the tree?

168
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:32,280
 Like is it going to be let us say three depth or four levels or five levels?

169
00:18:32,280 --> 00:18:36,280
 How much deep do you want to make the tree?

170
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:41,640
 Then the second question is the split criteria at the internal node.

171
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:48,360
 So we have a rule like earlier the point like where I was getting a little confused was because

172
00:18:48,360 --> 00:18:55,080
 of this comparison either the answer is yes or no and accordingly you would take one particular

173
00:18:55,080 --> 00:19:01,480
 root through the tree or path through the tree.

174
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:06,200
 So to determine which is going to be this rule or which is going to be the condition

175
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:12,200
 on which we are branching to different leaf nodes.

176
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:16,320
 So that itself is something which we will have to decide.

177
00:19:16,320 --> 00:19:23,760
 So that is the split criteria.

178
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:30,720
 So should we go for another classified to determine the split criteria or should we just do some

179
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:36,840
 simplest test to just identify whether we are doing it correctly or not.

180
00:19:36,840 --> 00:19:38,440
 What to do at the leaf node?

181
00:19:38,440 --> 00:19:44,880
 So leaf node is making the final decision but if all are all levels are the same like

182
00:19:44,880 --> 00:19:50,080
 if it is truly homogeneous then there is no problem but sometimes you may not be able to

183
00:19:50,080 --> 00:19:52,720
 distinguish it very cleanly.

184
00:19:52,720 --> 00:19:57,600
 So you may have a mix of two class levels even in a leaf node.

185
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:03,320
 So what to do in that case like should we make a constant prediction for each test input

186
00:20:03,320 --> 00:20:09,920
 which comes there or should we use something like a nearest neighbor based prediction but

187
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:14,840
 only looking at the inputs which are there at the leaf node.

188
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:20,880
 So these are many different options which we have.

189
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:25,360
 The question is which one to pick.

190
00:20:25,360 --> 00:20:31,040
 Often time cross validation which we discussed in the last class cross validation is typically

191
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:37,960
 used to decide many of these questions like whether we want to go with a particular size,

192
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:40,480
 we want to go through a particular depth.

193
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:49,360
 So all these things are typically decided based on the cross validation performance.

194
00:20:49,360 --> 00:20:56,800
 Now let us look at or let us spend a little bit more time on the split criteria what can

195
00:20:56,800 --> 00:21:03,600
 be done to figure out like how to split at internal nodes.

196
00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:10,700
 Now recall that root node deals with the entire training set when we come to the internal

197
00:21:10,700 --> 00:21:16,440
 nodes they typically receive a subset of all the training inputs.

198
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:23,440
 But our idea is that whatever the splitting criteria may be we would want a scenario where

199
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:30,600
 after the split it is made then in the next branch we want to reduce impurity as much

200
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:31,840
 as possible.

201
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:38,240
 So what is meant by impurity or on the other hand what is meant by a pure group or purity?

202
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:45,480
 A pure group means that most of the inputs for that group will have the same level or

203
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:47,280
 output.

204
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:50,380
 So let us just look at two examples.

205
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:58,360
 So let us say this was the internal node and in internal node we have four red data points

206
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:04,000
 and four blue data points so this is the distribution.

207
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:09,200
 So as you see that when the training data points came here you have equal number of

208
00:22:09,200 --> 00:22:13,440
 red data points equal number of blue data points.

209
00:22:13,440 --> 00:22:20,000
 Now suppose your criteria is such that it ended up having in the left hand side you ended

210
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:28,600
 up having two red nodes and two or let us say two examples which are red and two blue

211
00:22:28,600 --> 00:22:29,600
 examples.

212
00:22:29,600 --> 00:22:33,440
 On the right hand side also you have two red examples two blue examples.

213
00:22:33,440 --> 00:22:40,440
 So this is not a very good split because you started with a uniform distribution of class

214
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:46,840
 levels in that particular internal node if even after you split them if you end up getting

215
00:22:46,840 --> 00:22:54,560
 the same uniform distribution then there is no value of this splitting you are not helping

216
00:22:54,560 --> 00:23:01,240
 in the classification process rather compared to that a much better split is such which

217
00:23:01,240 --> 00:23:06,760
 even if it takes an uniform distribution of equal number of blue and red nodes but it

218
00:23:06,760 --> 00:23:13,960
 ends up dividing them in groups such that in each group either you get like a majority

219
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:20,560
 of red levels and a minority of blue levels or you get a majority of blue levels and a

220
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:22,940
 minority of red level.

221
00:23:22,940 --> 00:23:31,800
 So that way although you started with a uniform distribution you end up with a skewed distribution.

222
00:23:31,800 --> 00:23:38,240
 So this is actually considered as a good split and this intuition is utilized when we start

223
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:44,000
 building a decision tree.

224
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:47,440
 Now how do we measure this purity?

225
00:23:47,440 --> 00:23:53,840
 So purity is measured using a particular metric called entropy.

226
00:23:53,840 --> 00:23:56,460
 Entropy is a measure of purity.

